s put forward for  the uh dismantling  of the south vietnamese military  apparatus  when that was agreed to  they added to it the demand for the  dismantling of the south vietnamese  administrative apparatus  so  uh it is clear that what is being aimed  at is a substantial political takeover  now whether it is possible to avoid a  battle for saigon  uh it is too early to judge i would hope  and we certainly have attempted to  work in that direction  that such a battle can be avoided and it  is basically unnecessary because it  seems to us that the south vietnamese  government is prepared to draw the  conclusions  uh from the existing situation and in  fact was formed to correspond to the  demands of the communist side  secretary do you consider the united  states now  uh  it owes any any allegiance at all to the  paris factory now are we now bound in  any way by the paris agreements  well uh as far as the united states is  concerned  there aren't many provisions of the  paris agreement that are still  relevant  uh as far as the north vietnamese are  concerned they have stated that they  would like to that they  wish to carry out  the paris accord though by what  definition is not fully clear to me  we would certainly uh support this  if it has any meaning i ask one  follow-up  do you now favor  a uh american aide in the rebuilding of  north vietnam  uh  i consider no i do not favor american  aid for rebuilding north vietnam  well with respect to south vietnam we  will have to see what  uh  kind of government emerges and indeed  whether there's going to be a south  vietnam  uh  we  would certainly look at particular  specific humanitarian requests that can  be carried out by uh  by humanitarian agencies but we do  believe that the primary responsibility  should fall on  those who supplied the weapons for this  political  i'd like to ask a change about the  length of time that it took to complete  this evacuation yeah first the question  of whether  days went by after the end became  obvious before ordering the evacuation  second if after ordering it there was a  one-hour delay in helicopter landings  apparently caused by military confusion  third whether the evacuation was  prolonged  by picking up thousands of vietnamese  instead of concentrating on americans  and  fourth whether this was delayed even  further by ambassador martin's desire to  be the last man to leave the sinking  ship  in other words i i tried to put the  specifics in in order to ask you  did it take too long to get out of there  to write this last chapter  we got we got out  by uh  with all of  the personnel  that was there  without  without panic and without uh  the substantial uh  casualties that could have occurred  if civil order had totally broken down  we also managed to save 56 000 people  whose life was in the most severe  jeopardy  and  we  had to make a judgment  every day  how many people we thought we could  safely remove  without triggering a panic  and at the same time still be able to  carry out  our principal function  and the remaining functions  i think these objectives were achieved  and they were carried out successfully  and therefore i do not believe that  there was an undue delay  uh because an evacuation has been going  on for two weeks  the difference between the last stage  and the previous period was that the  last stage was done by  by helicopter  and the previous  stage had been done by fixed wing  uh  and  i think the ability to conduct the final  evacuation  uh by helicopter without casualties  during the operation  when at least casualties caused by  hostile action  is closely related to the uh  policies that were pursued in the  preceding  in the preceding two weeks  as for ambassador martin  he was in a very difficult position  uh he uh  he felt a moral obligation to the people  with whom he had been associated  and he attempted to save as many of  those as possible  uh and  that's not the worst ball the man can  have  there's been numerous reports of  american appeals to the soviets to the  chinese can you say today in the  evacuation effort where the  either the soviets or the chinese  helpful or unhelpful in this uh  diplomatic effort  uh  i think that  we received some help  from uh the soviet union in uh in the  evacuation effort  the degree of it uh we will  have to assess  when we study the exchanges  dr  what caused the breakdown of the attempt  which was spoken of earlier on the hill  to try to achieve a measure of  self-determination to the people of  south vietnam  and what is your total assessment now of  the  effectiveness of the non-effectiveness  of the whole paris support operation  which you said  at the outset uh was intended to achieve  peace with honor for the united states  the uh  until  until sunday night  we thought there was some  considerable hope  that  the north vietnamese would not seek a  solution by purely military means  and  when  the transfer of power to general min  took place  a person who had been designated by the  other side  as in as a counterpart  worth talking to who they would be  prepared to talk with  we thought that a negotiated solution  in the next few days  was highly profitable  sometime  sunday night  the north vietnamese obviously changed  signals  why that is we do not yet know  and nor do i exclude that now that the  american presence is totally removed  and uh  very little military structure is left  in south vietnam that there may not be  a sort of a negotiation  but what  uh what produced this  sudden shift to a military option  or what to seem to has to be a sudden  shift to a military option  i have not had a sufficient opportunity  to analyze  now as to the effectiveness of the paris  accords  i think it is important to remember  the mood in this country  uh at the time that the paris accords  were being negotiated  now i think it is worth remembering  that the principal criticism that was  then made  was that the terms we insisted on  uh were  were too tough  not that the terms  were too generous  we wanted what was considered peace with  honor was that the united states would  not end a war  by  overthrowing  a government with which it had been  associated  and  that still  seems like  uh an objective  that  that was correct  now there were several other assumptions  that were made at that time  that were later  falsified by events  that were beyond the control of and that  were indeed unforeseeable  by anybody who negotiated these  agreements  including the  disintegration of  uh  or the weakening of executive authority  in the united states  for reasons unconnected with foreign  policy considerations  so the premises of the paris occurred  in terms of aid of the possibility of  aid and in terms of other factors tended  to  [Music]  tended to disintegrate  i see no purpose now in  in reviewing that particular history  within the context of the time  uh  it seemed  the right thing to do  what is our what is the current  relationship of the united states  to the south vietnamese uh uh political  uh  grouping or whatever you would call it  we will have to we will have to see what  grouping emerges out of these [ __ ] out  of  whatever negotiations should now take  place  between the two south vietnamese sides  after we have seen what grouping emerges  and what degree of independence it has  then we can make a decision about what  our political relationship to it is we  have not made a decision on that you say  diplomatic relations are in abeyance  with the government in south vietnam i  think that's a fair statement  looking back on the war now  would you say that the war was in vain  and what do you feel at a conference  i think it will be a long time  before  we can  before americans will be able to talk or  write about the war with some dispassion  it is clear  that the war did not achieve the  objectives  of those  who who  started the original involvement  noah the objectives of those who sought  to end that involvement which they found  uh on terms which uh seem to them  compatible with the sacrifices that had  been made  what lessons we should draw from it i  think we should reserve for for another  occasion  but i don't think  that we can solve the problem  of having entered  the conflict too lightly  by leaving it too lightly either  secretary  looking toward the future has america  been so stunned by the experience in  vietnam that it will never again come to  the military or economic aid of an ally  i'm talking specifically in the case of  israel  well as i uh pointed out in a speech  a few uh a few weeks ago  it is one lesson we must learn from this  experience  is that we must be very careful  in the commitments we make  but uh that we should scrupulously honor  those commitments we do make  i believe that the experience in the war  that the war has had  can make us  more mature  in the commitments we undertake  and more determined to maintain those we  have  uh  i would therefore think that  with relation  to other countries  including israel  that no lessons should be drawn by  the enemies of our friends  from the experiences in vietnam  view of the developments  in the last week or so would you agree  that  there was never any hope of stabilizing  the south vietnamese military situation  after with the withdrawal from the  northern regions  now when uh the president met with  general ryan in palm springs  uh the judgment was that there was a  slim hope  but  somehow  uh  not  somewhat less than 50 50 but  still  some hope  the situation deteriorated with every uh  with every passing day  now those of you  whom i briefed at that time will  remember  said i said that whatever and i said it  in public uh testimony on innumerable  locations  that whatever objective  we  may set ourselves and whatever  assessment we make about the outcome  the administration had no choice except  to pursue the cause that we did  which was designed to save the american  still in vietnam and the maximum number  of  vietnamese lives should the birth come  to pass  mr secretary two questions could you  tell us are you now reassessing the  amount of humanitarian aid you wish  congress to give to the south vietnamese  and also can you tell us the president's  reaction and moods during these past 24  hours  uh  with respect to humanitarian aid for  south vietnam  we spoke to the congressional leadership  this morning  and we urged them  uh to uh  to pass the humanitarian  part of the eight requests that we have  submitted to the countries  the president pointed out that he would  make a later decision  as to  as to  what  uh  part of that humanitarian aid  could be  used in south vietnam  after the political evolution in south  vietnam becomes becomes clearer  the president's mood  was  somber  and  undetermined  and  we all went through  a somewhat anxious 24 hours  because until the last helicopter had  left  we  couldn't really know  uh  whether an attack on any of these  compounds might start  and whether  missiles might be used against our  evacuation  mr secretary can i ask you to clarify  something that seems rather important at  this point you've said here and in the  past that a weakening of the american  executive authority  was a factor in this whole outcome  now there have been reports that former  president nixon with your advice had  decided in april of 1973  to resume the bombing of north vietnam  but the watergate intruded and he could  not carry through that is that a  historic fact or not  uh  to  to my uh  to the best of my knowledge  uh president nixon had never actually  decided  on any particular action  the  washington special action group at that  period  was considering  a number of reactions  uh that could be taken to  the beginning flagrant violations  of uh  of the of the of the agreements and this  was done on a on an interdepartmental  basis including the department of state  my office the de